From 5ed2e533be73c2c5544c7e3b819e35472e527d0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MasherDon <62326358+MasherDon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 05:10:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/91] moe-stream : drop rule for light experts on the drive
 (lossy, opt-in); a degraded oracle to measure a predictor

Drop rule (LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_DROP_W=theta, off by default; proposed by the SiegeLadder user): a routed
expert held neither in VRAM nor whole in the host tier, whose share of its token's routing weight is below
theta in every pair it has, is not waited for. Its pairs get weight 0 for this call - a second custom op
right after the remap, on the same host split, hands the graph a weight mask - and it is read into the
host tier for the calls after it. LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_DROP_ALL=1 drops only when that frees the call from
the drive: a call with one heavy drive miss waits for the drive anyway, and dropping its light ones only
costs quality. LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_DROP_RENORM=1 scales the kept pairs back to the token's full weight. With
the rule on, the remap is a ggml_custom_4d op that also takes the routing weights.

Measurement apparatus for 3a: LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_ORACLE_RECALL keeps each expert of the oracle's staging
with that probability (a deterministic hash of call and expert); LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_PRED_STAGE=1 stages
only the model prediction's false positives on top of the oracle's staging, =2 computes the prediction
and stages nothing.

DeepSeek V4.1-Flash Q2mix, 512 tokens, -ub 1, 17 I/O threads, tok/s; KL against exact K=6 on ~3K
tokens at -ub 1 (SiegeLadder runs.md 4.27): base 9.3-10.0; drop < 0.12 10.8-11.2, KL 0.0137; with
DROP_ALL 10.6, KL 0.0105; DROP_ALL < 0.14 11.8-12.2, KL 0.0146; DROP_ALL < 0.14 with A' top 3 staged
12.4-12.5, KL 0.0166 (K=5: 0.0276). Oracle staging one call ahead at recall 1.0 / 0.9 / 0.75 / 0.6:
14.4 / 13.1 / 11.5 / 10.9; with A''s false positives at 0.6: 9.75, which is A' itself.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
